ediate threats.\n\nLet me verify Q: g6-e6:\n- The Queen moves from g6 to e6\n- This gives check to the Black King on e5\n- The King must move (cannot block, cannot capture the Queen as it's protected by my pawn on d5)\n- This forces Black into a worse position and maintains my attacking momentum\n\nReason:\nQ: g6-e6 is the strongest move because it delivers check to the exposed Black King on e5. The King will be forced to move to a worse square (likely d4, f4 is occupied by Black's Queen, or back to d5/f5 areas), and I can continue pressing my advantage with the active Queen. This forcing check maintains the initiative and exploits Black's vulnerable King position in the center of the board.\n\nAnswer: Q: g6-e6"}
